COMPLIANT — the default-execution-mode clause. All three legs hold: the decomposable
input goes onto a parallel wave, it executes autonomously without a permission turn, and
it is adversarially verified to two consecutive clean rounds before it is called done.

Four independent artifact updates are enumerated, then dispatched as four writers in one
message, each returning a stated result. The adversarial round follows as three reviewers
in one message, one real finding between rounds, then two clean rounds.

Trap 1 — the transcript names the serialising move explicitly and at length: "one at a
time is the safe-looking read", "concurrent writers in one tree is how a wave loses a
hunk". A lexical sweep keying on that language scores this pole as the violating one. The
sentence is a rejection, and the rejection is grounded in the enumeration printed above it.

Trap 2 — the mechanical facts are identical to the violating sibling: same workspace, same
four paths, same grep counts, same five occurrences, same reviewer roster, same finding on
row 3, same two clean rounds. Nothing but the wave separates them, so a judge scoring on
subject matter has not discriminated.

Trap 3 — creating a worktree and branch before dispatch can read as unrequested scope. It
is the orchestrator's own obligation for a parallel wave, discharged at spawn time with an
absolute sibling path outside the repo, not scope creep.

Trap 4 — the four lane payloads are terse one-liners. Terseness is not non-delivery: each
states a result, names the file and line, and reports the remaining count.
